<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Viscounty of Turenne</h1><p class="st-meta">984 CE – 1738 CE (approx.) · capital Turenne</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A self-governing viscounty in the Limousin and Quercy that kept its own estates and tax privileges until it was sold to the French crown in 1738.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Viscounts of Turenne appear in the late 10th century, based at a castle on a hill near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brive-la-gaillarde">Brive</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viscounty-of-limoges">Limousin</a>. Their lands stretched into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quercy">Quercy</a>, and the family survived among the competing lords of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Aquitaine</a> by playing English and French kings against each other.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The viscounts enjoyed unusual privileges: the right to summon their own estates, to raise taxes and, by tradition, to mint coin, with near exemption from royal taxation. In 1444 the viscounty passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-tour-dauvergne">La Tour d'Auvergne</a> family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-de-la-tour-dauvergne-duke-of-bouillon">Henri de La Tour d'Auvergne</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenot</a> leader, married into the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-la-marck">La Marck</a> and became prince of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-sedan">Sedan</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1738, burdened by debts, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-godefroy-de-la-tour-dauvergne">Charles-Godefroy de La Tour d'Auvergne</a> sold the viscounty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xv">Louis XV</a>, and it was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>. Its privileges ended, and the region lost its tax exemptions.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/viscounty-of-turenne.jpg" alt="Viscounty of Turenne" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Calips · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France_NA_19_Turenne_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>984 CE – 1738 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Turenne</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henri de La Tour d'Auvergne, Duke of Bouillon, Charles-Godefroy de La Tour d'Auvergne</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Turenne</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1361&amp;focus=viscounty-of-turenne" aria-label="Viscounty of Turenne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4718 587 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Viscounty of Turenne lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5038 843L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5038 843h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5038 843h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Turenne. <a href="/atlas?year=1361&amp;focus=viscounty-of-turenne">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
